01:31.000 --> 01:35.000
And we also learned about trace backs and exceptions.

01:35.000 --> 01:44.000
So you've probably already seen the indentation error quite a few times as you've been working in Python.

01:44.000 --> 01:48.000
You've also seen the more general syntax error.

01:48.000 --> 01:52.000
There are other common exceptions that you might see.

01:52.000 --> 01:56.000
For example, if you try to divide by zero, you will see a zero division error.

01:56.000 --> 02:02.000
Or if you try to get a key out of a dictionary and that key doesn't exist, you will see a key error.

02:02.000 --> 02:07.000
Adding together to unrelated types will throw an exception as well.

02:07.000 --> 02:13.000
If you want to raise your own exceptions, you will need to inherit from the exception class.

02:13.000 --> 02:23.000
You do that by when you define your class passing in the exception class in parentheses and then you can go ahead and just raise it.

02:23.000 --> 02:31.000
Having a custom exceptions in your programs is a great best practice because we want to catch exceptions that are specific as possible.

02:31.000 --> 02:38.000
So that we have a better idea of what's going wrong when things do inevitably go wrong.

02:38.000 --> 02:42.000
You can catch exceptions with try and accept.

02:42.000 --> 02:49.000
So try followed by an indented block of code with what you are trying that might throw an exception.

02:49.000 --> 02:55.000
Followed by an accept clause that also defines what exception you would like to catch.

02:55.000 --> 03:03.000
You can also use that as keyword if you need a handle on that exception, for example, if you wanted to grab the error message.

03:03.000 --> 03:17.000
You can also re-raise exceptions as needed, so catching an exception and then raising a more specific one or catching an exception and then raising a custom exception are both things that you might see in Python code.

